{"author":"Unknown","author_email":"","data":{"content_hash":"908644f4b4c21a50169de8e6705de8b76321ad1a7c3d4eb75192cd37e21ea4c5","digest":{"component_or_area":"compactor process-death recovery","key_entities":["rebar compact","src/rebar/_commands/compact.py","tests/interfaces/lifecycle/","write lock","source UUID","fsck"],"problem_keywords":["process death recovery","compaction durability","SIGKILL resilience","lock reclamation","source retirement","snapshot consistency"],"propositions":["Existing compaction tests only mock Python exceptions, not actual process death (SIGKILL), leaving the real durability contract unproven.","A real SIGKILL of the rebar compact subprocess skips cleanup code (rollback, lock-release, cache-cleanup, git-staging), leaving a dirty tracker worktree between snapshot write and commit.","After deterministic process death (via filesystem barrier), a fresh CLI process must recover the write lock, restore the ticket to its pre-crash semantic state, and preserve all source UUIDs.","Re-running compaction after process death must be idempotent or perform documented repair, with fsck returning clean/repairable result."]},"digest_hash_version":1,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8","schema":"ticket_digest_v1"},"env_id":"b2b5c498-2f67-4ae3-bafc-eca40294bec2","event_type":"TICKET_DIGEST","timestamp":1785819535894466129,"uuid":"34ac789d-e4ad-4e6d-86c9-0706abbebec3"}